The general shape, independent of what the signature is: for each item, compute its signature, then append the item to `groups[signature]` (creating an empty list first if the signature hasn't been seen). One pass, O(n) items processed, each append O(1) amortized.

```python
def group_by(items, signature_fn):
    groups = {}
    for item in items:
        key = signature_fn(item)
        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(item)
    return list(groups.values())
```

Everything in this topic is this same function with a different `signature_fn` plugged in — sorted string, count tuple, digit sum, remainder, or a custom shape. Recognizing that the loop body never changes, only the key computation does, is the actual transferable skill.

```python
def group_anagrams(strs):
    groups = {}
    for s in strs:
        key = ''.join(sorted(s))       # canonical signature
        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(s)
    return list(groups.values())

# ["eat","tea","tan","ate","nat","bat"]
# -> [["eat","tea","ate"], ["tan","nat"], ["bat"]]
```

Sorting each string is O(m log m) for a string of length m, so with n strings the total is O(n · m log m) — the sort inside the loop, not the grouping itself, is the dominant cost. The grouping map is still just O(n) work on top of that.
